---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const pageTitle = "The Loop — FOMO & Social Comparison";
const description =
  "How curated social media content amplifies fear of missing out, shapes self-worth, and affects young adults’ relationships, body image, and sense of achievement.";
---

<MainLayout title={pageTitle} description={description}>
  <section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-4xl flex-col gap-12 px-4 py-12">

    <!-- Intro -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        FOMO & Social Comparison
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          You open a social media app and see friends at a party you weren’t invited to.
          Someone else just landed an internship. Another person is travelling, training,
          or “living their best life”.
        </p>

        <p>
          Even if you were perfectly content a moment ago, that feeling can shift quickly.
          A quiet sense that you’re falling behind. That something important is happening
          without you.
        </p>

        <p>
          This experience is often described as <strong>FOMO</strong> — the fear of missing out.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Definition -->
    <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
      <p>
        FOMO refers to the anxiety that others are having rewarding experiences while you
        are absent. On social media, it is closely tied to
        <strong>social comparison</strong> — the tendency to evaluate yourself by comparing
        your life, appearance, or achievements to those of others.
      </p>

      <p>
        While social comparison is not new, social platforms intensify it by making
        comparison constant, visual, and measurable.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Amplification -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        How platforms amplify FOMO
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Stories</h4>

          <p>
            Stories are designed to be temporary. They disappear after 24 hours, which
            creates a sense of urgency: if you don’t check now, you might miss something.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because stories often show social events, trips, or daily highlights, they
            invite constant comparison between others’ curated moments and your own
            everyday reality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Likes and visible metrics</h4>

          <p>
            Likes, views, follower counts, and reactions turn social interaction into
            something measurable.
          </p>

          <p>
            These metrics make comparison effortless. It’s no longer just “who looks
            happier”, but “who gets more attention”, reinforcing feelings of inadequacy
            or social pressure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Highlight reels</h4>

          <p>
            Most users post moments they are proud of — achievements, attractive photos,
            exciting experiences. Failures, boredom, and insecurity rarely make it into
            the feed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over time, this creates the illusion that everyone else is constantly succeeding,
            while you are the only one struggling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Event-based notifications</h4>

          <p>
            Notifications such as “X is live”, “Your friend just posted”, or “Don’t miss this”
            encourage immediate checking.
          </p>

          <p>
            These prompts reinforce the idea that important social moments are happening
            right now — and that being offline means being left out.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Impact -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        Impact on young adult life
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Body image</h4>

          <p>
            Repeated exposure to idealised bodies and lifestyles can distort how young
            adults perceive themselves. Fitness, beauty, and lifestyle content often
            becomes a reference point rather than an exception.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Social comparison</h4>

          <p>
            Constant comparison can lower self-esteem and increase feelings of inadequacy,
            especially when users measure their behind-the-scenes reality against others’
            public highlights.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Academic and career pressure</h4>

          <p>
            Seeing peers announce achievements, internships, or career milestones can
            intensify pressure to “keep up”, even when life paths and circumstances differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Relationship anxiety</h4>

          <p>
            Social media can create uncertainty around relationships — who is spending
            time with whom, who is excluded, or whether a relationship appears as happy
            as others online.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Case Study: Instagram -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        Case study — Instagram Explore & body image
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          Studies have repeatedly linked intensive Instagram use to lower self-esteem and
          increased body dissatisfaction, particularly among young women.
        </p>

        <p>
          Instagram’s Explore page plays a key role in this process. The algorithm often
          recommends fitness, beauty, lifestyle, and influencer content because it
          performs well in terms of engagement.
        </p>

        <p>
          Once a user interacts with this type of content, similar posts are surfaced more
          frequently, creating a feedback loop where idealised bodies and lifestyles
          dominate the feed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ethically, this raises concerns about amplified comparison loops — systems that
          repeatedly expose users to content known to affect self-worth, without accounting
          for mental health consequences.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Case Study: BeReal -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        Case study — BeReal & real-time pressure
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          BeReal positions itself as an alternative to curated social media, encouraging
          users to post unfiltered photos once per day at a random time.
        </p>

        <p>
          However, the “post now or miss out” mechanic introduces a different form of
          social pressure. Users are encouraged to be constantly available, alert, and
          ready to participate.
        </p>

        <p>
          The result is a form of real-time FOMO: anxiety about missing the moment,
          appearing inactive, or failing to keep up with friends.
        </p>

        <p>
          While the platform aims to reduce performativity, it still encourages frequent
          checking and reinforces the idea that social presence must be continuous.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

  </section>
</MainLayout>
